    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Honda Racing Releases Its First-Ever In-House Made Parts for the Civic Type R and Integra Type S\nCanadian 1971 Corvette 454 Rocks Holy Grail LS6 and Automatic Combo, It's Rarer Than Dragon Eggs\n51 photos\nPhoto: Mecum\nWhen the 1970 model year rolled around, America's premier sports car suffered an unexpected humiliation inside its own showroom. The Chevrolet Corvette failed to claim the top-dog spot in the division's performance roster, surrendering the horsepower crown to the mid-size Chevelle SS 454 LS6 and its 450-hp rating. That internal upset happened because the Bowtie brand abruptly retired the planned 454-cubic-inch LS7 V8 and its 460-hp output from the catalog.\nChevrolet rectified that hierarchy imbalance in 1971 by finally dropping the legendary LS6 big-block motor between the front fenders of the Stingray. However, tightening federal emissions standards and the corporate mandate for engines to run on 91-octane low-lead fuel forced engineers to lower the compression ratio to 9.0:1. It was quite the drop, from the 11.25 squeeze of 1970, and yet it still punched hard.\nConsequently, the 454-cubic-inch powerplant arrived slightly detuned to 425 gross hp (325 net hp), while still generating 475 lb-ft of gross torque (down from 500).\nEquipped with lightweight aluminum cylinder heads, solid lifters, forged aluminum pistons, and an 850-cfm Holley four-barrel carburetor, the LS6 remained an absolute monster – in more than one way. The ugly side of having access to pavement-wrinkling cubic inches of performance was quantified in rectangular paper portraits of dead American presidents. Or, in certain cases, legal tender snapshots of the queen. Queen of England. More on that in a minute.\nPhoto: Mecum Detroit priced the LS6 at $1,221. A lot of money, considering the base Corvette started at $5,496 (coupe), or $5,259 (ragtop). The perfect explanation for scarcity, and the perfect reason to rejoyce when seeing one in the flesh.\nOne pristine example of this brief big-block era is the car in the gallery, chassis 194371S118779, a Classic White coupe that was sold new in Canada, and spent its first 52 years in the Great White North, in Quebec, Canada.\nAccording to General Motors of Canada and Vintage Vehicle Services documentation, the coupe rolled off the St. Louis, Missouri assembly line on June 7, 1971. It shipped the following day to J.P. Charbonneau Autos Ltee in Montreal. It was 1 of 823 examples of its model number imported for sale in Canada that year, carrying a total factory sticker price of $8,760.80, including $2,132.35 in options. That’s CANADIAN dollars, hence the queen reference from earlier.\nPhoto: Mecum The car remained in its home province for decades and was acquired in 1994 by a caretaker from Trois-Rivières, Quebec, who kept it for 28 years. Beginning in 2018, that long-term owner commissioned a comprehensive four-year refurbishment to return the aging big-block coupe to showroom glory. The bodywork received a fresh coat of code-972 Classic White paint, while the cabin retained its code-412 Dark Blue vinyl bucket seats and trim.\nIn January 2023, the Canadian owner parted ways with the freshly refurbished Stingray on Bring a Trailer, where it sold for $135,000 with approximately 36,874 miles showing on its odometer. The winning bidder brought the Corvette back to the United States, where it was briefly marketed by Paramount Classic Cars in Hickory, North Carolina.\nIt subsequently entered the care of another enthusiast who pursued top-tier historical validation and judging. In January of this year, it crossed the Mecum auction block at the Kissimmee event. It hammered for $102,500.\nPhoto: Mecum Those efforts paid off handsomely during the 2026 judging season. In May 2026, the coupe earned a National Corvette Restorers Society Top Flight Award at the Wisconsin Chapter meet, achieving a remarkable net score of 95.7 out of 100. Less than a month later, on June 13, 2026, the National Corvette Certification Board awarded the white big-block its coveted Bloomington Gold Certification, confirming its exceptional preservation and factory-correct mechanical execution.\nWhat makes this Canadian-delivered coupe so desirable is the extreme scarcity of its drivetrain. The LS6 engine was a one-year-only option for the Corvette, offered exclusively during the 1971 model year before vanishing forever. Out of 21,801 total Corvettes manufactured that year, buyers ordered just 188 examples with the $1,221 LS6 engine option.\nThat tiny figure makes the 1971 LS6 rarer than the famous L88 big-block across its multi-year run. To be fair, there was a distinct batch of a dozen units equipped with the same 454-cube LS6, sold under the ZR2 “Special Purpose Engine Package.” It doesn’t take the IQ of a quantum physicist to determine what that purpose was, but the price of admission was matching: $1,747. So, a grand total of 176 “normal” LS6 Vettes were made and sold during the 1971 model year.\nPhoto: Mecum Breaking down those cheaper LS6s by body style reveals that the vast majority mirrored this Classic White example. While total 1971 production saw 14,680 T-top coupes outnumber 7,121 convertibles by more than 2 to 1, the LS6 split was similarly skewed. Historians and registry experts account for 138 coupes and only 50 convertibles equipped with the 425-hp V8, making either body style a prized catch for serious American muscle collectors.\nThe transmission breakdown adds another layer of intrigue to the 1971 LS6 story. The ZR2 Special Purpose LS6 Engine Package, which mandated the heavy-duty M22 Rock Crusher 4-speed manual gearbox. Because Chevrolet produced 130 total M22 transmissions in 1971—with 8 going to small-block ZR1s—110 regular LS6 Corvettes received the M22.\nThe remaining 66 cars without the heavy-duty M22 were split almost evenly between the no-cost M21 close-ratio 4-speed manual and the M40 3-speed Turbo Hydra-Matic 400 automatic. Notably, 1971 marked the first time Chevrolet allowed buyers to pair a solid-lifter big-block engine with an automatic gearbox.\nPhoto: Mecum Of the roughly 33 automatic LS6 Corvettes built, only 7 were convertibles, leaving around 26 automatic coupes like this Montreal-delivered time capsule. Holy Grail? It’s safe to say it can snuggly fit that bill. Speaking of bills, the window sticker presents a short, but fat list of options:\nEquipped with that rare M40 automatic transmission, a 3.36:1 Positraction rear axle, J50 vacuum power brakes, N40 hydraulic power steering, A31 power windows, and a U79 AM/FM stereo with a 4-speaker system, this Stingray cost $8,760.80 CAD when new, of which $2,132.35 were the add-ons (the engine alone cost $1,365.85).\nUsually, cars like this go for $150k USD or more at auctions, making this particular white example an anomaly. The odometer reads 36,904 miles (so it covered 30 miles in the last 46 months), and I only have one more question about it: what’s the high bid going to be at the Mecum Kansas City auction on December 5? If you have any thoughts, drop them in the comments.\nVia // Mecum\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nCorvette LS6 Corvette Chevrolet Corvette 1971 chevrolet corvette 454 ls6 sportscar Chevrolet 454 LS6\nAbout the author: Razvan Calin\nAfter nearly two decades in news television, Răzvan turned to a different medium. He’s been a field journalist, a TV producer, and a seafarer but found that he feels right at home among petrolheads. Full profile\nUP NEXT Flat-12 Awesomeness: 1970 Porsche 917K Parades Gulf Livery at Imola",
